  "narrative": "Independent validation changes the scientific picture materially. For H96, the claim that CDKN2A loss among TP53-intact cells is associated with much stronger MDM2 dependency is now independently validated. The signed estimate for the registered direction is +0.122 in the validation split, with an independent 95% interval from +0.064 to +0.180 and cell sizes 321 and 1274. Using the absolute mean of dependency_MDM2 as the denominator for the 10 percent practical benchmark, the threshold is about 0.0269, so even the adjusted lower bound is more than two-fold above the clinically meaningful benchmark. Because positive signed values correspond, for this outcome, to a more negative raw CRISPR dependency score, CDKN2A-loss cells appear to have stronger MDM2 dependency when TP53 is intact. This is now one of the strongest supported associations in the supplied dataset. It remains an association in the supplied validation split, not evidence of causality or external biological generalizability. The earlier discovery and subgroup robustness checks (non-Avana, adenocarcinoma, and low-9p21 copy-number stratum) align with the same direction and magnitude. H67, NF1 loss among KRAS-wildtype cells, is explicitly rejected and closed. Its discovery estimate was borderline and not robust across uncertainty, and the independent validation estimate is essentially null: +0.0005 with an interval from -0.042 to +0.043. This does not support NF1 loss as a meaningful bypass or modifier of KRAS dependency in the tested KRAS-wildtype population. Therefore the main validated supported claims are SMARCA4 loss with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency, TP53 loss with much weaker USP7 dependency, KEAP1 loss with much weaker NFE2L2 dependency, MSI-high with much stronger WRN dependency, and CDKN2A loss with much stronger MDM2 dependency specifically in TP53-intact cells. The next most informative analyses should localize the CDKN2A/MDM2 finding rather than broaden into low-prior predictors. I propose a direct TP53-loss complement mean difference and an interaction contrast by TP53-loss status. If CDKN2A loss is associated with stronger MDM2 dependency in TP53-intact cells but not in TP53-loss cells, that would support pathway specificity through the intact p53 regulatory axis, though still only as an association. The active MTAP-loss proposals H106 and H107 are also worth prioritizing because they test whether nearby 9p21 co-deletion markers add to the CDKN2A/MDM2 signal. H42 remains useful for completing KEAP1/NFE2L2 robustness in the EGFR-amplification context. Broad EGFR, KRAS, MYC, STAT3, and RIT1 bypass screens are lower priority unless they become mechanistically connected to these validated signals.",
  "research_notes": "Post-validation synthesis: H96 is accepted and closed as an independently validated in-supplied-dataset association. Signed validation estimate +0.122, independent interval +0.064 to +0.180, far above MDM2 10 percent benchmark ~0.0269. Interpretation: CDKN2A loss with TP53-intact status is associated with stronger MDM2 dependency (raw MDM2 score more negative), not causal and not externally generalized beyond supplied split. H67 rejected/closed after validation: near-zero validation estimate +0.0005, interval -0.042 to +0.043, no meaningful NF1-loss/KRAS-wildtype bypass. Strong accepted set: SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2; TP53 loss -> weaker USP7; KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2; MSI-high -> stronger WRN; CDKN2A loss/TP53-intact -> stronger MDM2. Next priorities: test CDKN2A/MDM2 specificity in TP53-loss complement and TP53-loss interaction; analyze active MTAP-loss tests H106/H107; complete H42 KEAP1/NFE2L2 in EGFR-amplified cells. Deprioritize broad predictor screens unless they refine validated mechanisms."
}
